PRINCIPLES
* Adoption is a pipeline of stages. Each stage: precheck -> backup -> apply -> verify -> report to the server. Any failure stops the pipeline and rolls back.
* Every object we create has a fixed name prefix and the comment "tconnect:managed". Reruns, cleanup and drift detection touch only our objects.
* Safety net: before any change, save a binary backup and an export off the router, and arm a revert timer that restores the pre-adoption state unless the server confirms (commit) over the tunnel within N minutes. Verify the mechanism per RouterOS docs and prove it in AT-28.
* Detached execution: the adoption script runs as a detached job, so it completes even when the admin's session on the old LAN address drops after the subnet change.
* No permanent local management on the guest bridge. Break-glass local WinBox/SSH is enabled only by an audited admin action, time-limited (default 15 min). The reset button is the last resort.
* GREENFIELD (factory-fresh or reset) is the default and is built and tested first. BROWNFIELD (router already in service) is read-only discovery, exact diff, admin approval, managed objects only; it refuses by default on conflicts until built and tested.
* Re-adoption: factory reset + the one-liner restores the same router identity and tunnel IP.
* The script inspector shows the full generated script and stores it as a config_snapshot. Viewing is always available; mandatory approval applies to brownfield only.

